>
  <header class="doc-head">
    <h1>Paths &amp; MSYS: the biggest behavioral difference</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      For every predecessor of "bash on Windows", paths are the compulsory question: either rewrite
      arguments heuristically before spawn like Git Bash, or force a new <code>/mnt/c</code> dialect
      like WSL. niubash's answer is a third one: <strong>all four ecosystem path dialects are accepted
      and genuinely folded, while argv itself arrives byte-for-byte verbatim</strong> — because path
      translation is a table-driven engine responsibility, not a compatibility layer hiding inside
      child processes.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Four dialects, one engine accepts them all</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Dialect</th><th>Example</th><th>Folding behavior</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Windows native</td>
          <td><code>C:\repo</code>, <code>C:/repo</code></td>
          <td>First-class citizen — the native form is the shell's internal representation; there is nothing to "convert to".</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>MSYS / Git Bash form</td>
          <td><code>/d/repo</code>, <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/usr/bin</code></td>
          <td>Native children receive <code>D:\repo</code>; <code>/usr/bin</code>, <code>/etc</code>, <code>/tmp</code> map to real Windows directories under the install root. Exception: <code>cmd.exe</code> receives <code>/d/repo</code> verbatim (<code>/d</code> is indistinguishable from its own switches).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>WSL form</td>
          <td><code>/mnt/d/repo</code></td>
          <td><strong>Genuinely folded</strong> to <code>D:\repo</code> (<code>pwd -P</code> reports <code>/d/repo</code>). Contrast: Git Bash's <code>/mnt/d</code> is a false positive — it silently lands in the install root <code>D:\Git\mnt\d</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cygwin form</td>
          <td><code>/cygdrive/d/repo</code></td>
          <td>Fully accepted at the external-command layer; the builtin-side branch is being completed (rubash#850).</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The translation rules are <strong>table-driven and context-free</strong> (rubash#600): no
      guessing "does this argument look like a path", no looking at argument position, no checking
      whether the target exists — paths about to be created also get a legal Win32 form. The shipped
      PATH-MATRIX runs ~100 probes across the full dialect × execution-layer matrix, and every gap
      has a filed ticket (WinuxCmd#1244, rubash#850, niu-git#18).
    </p>
    <details class="zh-fold">
      <summary>中文原文 · Chinese original</summary>
      <div lang="zh-CN">
        <p>
          翻译规则是<strong>表驱动且与上下文无关</strong>的（rubash#600）：不猜「这个参数
          像不像路径」、不看参数位置、不看目标存在与否——即将创建的路径也得到合法的
          Win32 形态。随包的 PATH-MATRIX 以约 100 条探针逐格实跑了方言 × 执行层的全量
          矩阵，缺口全部开票在案（WinuxCmd#1244、rubash#850、niu-git#18）。
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>argv byte-for-byte verbatim: MSYS_NO_PATHCONV does not exist here</h2>
    <p>
      Git Bash's MSYS runtime <strong>heuristically rewrites</strong> path-looking arguments before
      spawn: <code>/c</code> becomes <code>C:\</code>, a non-existent <code>/foo</code> expands into
      the Git install path, the pattern in <code>git grep "/fn main"</code> gets silently rewritten.
      That is why every veteran user has had to learn the <code>MSYS_NO_PATHCONV</code> /
      <code>MSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL</code> incantations — <strong>the existence of an off switch is itself
      the official admission of the problem</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      niubash has no MSYS runtime; those two environment variables <strong>do not exist here and do
      not need to</strong>. The boundary is a written core invariant — "what must be verbatim is not
      converted": commit messages, branch names, quoted operands, the syntax data of
      grep/sed/awk/find (patterns, script text, <code>-name</code>-style values),
      <code>/cygdrive/…</code>, <code>/dev/*</code>, the environment-variable channel, every
      non-path-shaped word — all reach the child process byte-for-byte. The only switch,
      <code>__RUBASH_ARGV_DIALECT=legacy</code>, is a documented dialect selector (an emergency
      fallback to the old behavior), not a life raft for "heuristics that might guess wrong".
    </p>
    <details class="zh-fold">
      <summary>中文原文 · Chinese original</summary>
      <div lang="zh-CN">
        <p>
          <code>$PWD</code> 恒为 POSIX 形态（如 <code>/d/repo</code>）是配套裁决：这是
          nvm 死循环根因（rubash#537）修复的同一决定，生态脚本对 <code>$PWD</code> 的
          POSIX 假设因此成立。
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>uname reporting MSYS_NT: a deliberate compatibility persona</h2>
    <p>
      On Windows, <code>uname -s</code> reports <code>MSYS_NT-…</code> (mapped to
      <code>MINGW64_NT-</code> and friends in the MSYS2 manner when <code>MSYSTEM</code> is set),
      with <code>$OSTYPE=msys</code> and <code>$MACHTYPE=x86_64-pc-msys</code>. This is not an
      impersonation of a runtime but a <strong>compatibility persona</strong> (rubash#154): plenty of
      ecosystem scripts use <code>case "$(uname -s)"</code> matching <code>MINGW*|MSYS*|CYGWIN*</code>
      as their branching gate, and the persona opens those gates so scripts take their most-tested
      paths. Under the label there is <strong>no MSYS2 runtime at all</strong>: no
      <code>msys-2.0.dll</code>, no <code>cygwin1.dll</code>, no POSIX argv conversion layer inside
      child processes.
    </p>
    <p>
      One-line contrast: <strong>Git Bash = MSYS2 runtime + real bash; niubash = native engine +
      compatibility persona.</strong> The persona is disclosed everywhere (<code>--help</code> /
      <code>--identity</code>), and <code>RUBASH_IDENTITY=native</code> switches to the honest native
      face (<code>uname -s → Windows_NT</code>, <code>OSTYPE=windows</code>).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Honesty section: the write-side gap, tracked in the open</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        A boundary contract is not zero gaps. <strong>The bare-drive-root gap in write-side
        commands</strong> is being closed: #1230 audited 15 write-side commands, with
        <code>mkdir</code>'s true write-mode support (#1233) the most urgent. That is the value of a
        boundary contract — gaps are enumerable, ticketable and fixable, instead of scattered in the
        fog of "sometimes it behaves like Linux, sometimes not". Every cell closed turns another
        PATH-MATRIX cell green.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

